/* Red-path meta-scenario 2 of 2: a run that DIES.

   The other half of the verdict proof.  A failed check is a result the script
   chose to record; a throw is the script losing control entirely, and the two
   travel different paths through the runner - one through the checks list, one
   through the catch.  Both must end at a non-zero exit code.

   It also proves the post-mortem: the check below is recorded BEFORE the throw,
   so the result file must come back holding that green check AND the #error
   trailer.  A run that died with its evidence lost would be far less useful.

   IllegalStateException is java.lang, so naming it unqualified reaches none of
   the sandbox's forbidden patterns - no import, no dotted name, no restricted
   runtime class. */
checkTrue("recorded-before-the-throw", true);
throw new IllegalStateException("boom");
